     bbcc is a language that performs calculations on numbers with
     an arbitrary number of digits.  It is most commonly used as
     an interactive calculator, where the user types arithmetic
     expressions in a syntax reminiscent of C.

     bbcc recognizes one option:

     -ll   Read the extended math library /uussrr/lliibb/lliibb.bb before
          reading any file or standard input.

     After bbcc reads each named _f_i_l_e, it reads the standard input.
     This lets you load a program stored in a file and then con-
     tinue typing at the terminal.

     The delimiters /* and */ enclose comments.  The three built-
     in variables oobbaassee, iibbaassee, and ssccaallee give, respectively, the
     number base used for printing numbers (default: ten), the
     number base used for reading numbers (default: ten), and the
     number of digits kept after the decimal point (default:
     zero).  Variables may be simple variables or arrays, and
     need not be declared, except for variables local to a func-
     tion.

